6. Combat Hints

Objective

Introduce a new floating combat feedback system, visually similar to damage hints, but displaying short contextual text instead of numerical damage.

The new Combat hints option should control this system independently of Damage hints.

There are three required messages:

* Blocked
* Deflected
* Stunned

Do not introduce unnecessary additional messages.

Use event-driven combat feedback rather than continuously displaying text based on a fighter’s current animation.

6.1 — “Blocked”

Trigger: A fighter successfully blocks an incoming attack.

Display:

Blocked

Position: Directly above the fighter who blocked the attack.

Examples:

* The player blocks a CPU melee attack with their Shield → Blocked appears above the player.
* The CPU blocks a projectile → Blocked appears above the CPU.
* A perfect Shield successfully blocks an attack → Blocked appears above the defender.

Use the combat system’s actual resolved block event:

CombatSystem.applyHit() in js/game/combat/combat.js already produces events with type: 'block'.

Requirements:

* Display the hint only when the combat system confirms a block.
* Position it over event.target.
* Include perfect blocks, without needing a separate message.
* Support melee, projectile, summon, and technique attacks.
* Do not trigger on a Shield being raised without contact.
* Do not trigger when an attack misses.
* Do not trigger when a fighter takes an ordinary hit.

If repeated attacks are blocked rapidly, stack or separate the messages so they remain legible.

6.2 — “Deflected”

Trigger: A projectile or other supported deflectable object is successfully redirected by a deflection mechanic.

Display:

Deflected

Position: Directly above the object that was deflected.

This placement is particularly important.

The text must not simply appear above the fighter performing the Deflect.

For example:

* Fighter #0001 performs an aerial Deflect against an incoming projectile.
* The projectile is successfully redirected.
* Deflected appears immediately above that projectile at the moment of redirection.

Existing engine integration

The relevant system already exists:

js/game/combat/combat.js

Specifically:

* CombatSystem.deflectProjectiles()
* CombatSystem.deflections

Each confirmed deflection already records information about the deflecting fighter and the redirected projectile.

Use these records.

Also inspect js/game/combat/projectile.js, particularly Projectile.turnBack() and clashProjectiles(), because some projectile abilities can redirect other projectiles through repelling mechanics.

Where an interaction genuinely redirects a projectile, it should be eligible for the same feedback. Simple projectile collisions, erasures, or destruction must not be labeled as deflections.

Rendering requirements

* Anchor the message to the actual projectile’s position.
* Use the existing world-to-screen camera conversion.
* Position the text slightly above the projectile sprite.
* Do not use the deflecting fighter’s name tag as the anchor.
* Avoid displaying duplicate messages for the same redirection.
* Support multiple projectiles being deflected in quick succession.
* Ensure the message can finish its animation safely if the projectile subsequently disappears.

For the most reliable behavior, capture the deflected object’s position at the moment of the event, then animate the hint from that position. Following the projectile briefly while it remains valid is acceptable, but disappearing or destroyed entities must never cause errors.

6.3 — “Stunned”

Trigger: A fighter receives a genuine disabling status effect that temporarily prevents them from acting.

Display:

Stunned

Position: Directly above the fighter receiving the effect.

Critical distinction

Ordinary hitstun must NOT trigger the Stunned hint.

Alva already has separate combat state fields:

* combat.stun — ordinary hitstun.
* combat.shieldStun — blockstun.
* combat.paralysis — an actual immobilizing status effect.

The Stunned hint is intended for exceptional disabling effects such as paralysis, not normal attack reactions.

Example — Fighter #0001

Fighter #0001’s Unlimited Void technique applies a paralysis effect.

When that technique successfully paralyzes the opponent:

* Display Stunned above the affected fighter.
* Only trigger when the paralysis actually applies.
* Do not display it if the attack is blocked.
* Do not display it merely because the fighter is in a hurt animation.
* Do not continuously spawn new Stunned messages for every simulation frame the paralysis remains active.

The combat system already exposes the resolved paralysis value in hit events and stores the status in CombatState.

Use those existing values.

Future compatibility

Avoid hardcoding fighter #0001 or Unlimited Void into the rendering system.

The correct behavior is:

Any fighter who receives a supported, genuine incapacitating effect should receive the Stunned hint.

This makes the system compatible with future fighters and abilities.

Trigger when the status is newly applied or meaningfully reapplied, not on every frame it remains active.

Do not show Stunned for:

* Ordinary hitstun.
* Shield blockstun.
* Knockback.
* Tumbling.
* Normal attack recovery.
* Cooldown restrictions.
* A fighter simply standing still.
* Non-incapacitating effects.

