12. Final Acceptance Criteria

The implementation is complete only when all the following are true:

1. Launch Point numbers glow green during active passive recovery and return to normal when recovery is interrupted or completed.
2. A fourth settings category called Interface appears after Combat.
3. All four interface options can be enabled and disabled independently.
4. Every preference saves correctly and survives reloading the game.
5. Disabling Ability HUD completely hides the ability display without affecting player input.
6. Disabling cooldown indicators hides the under-character cooldown visuals without affecting actual cooldowns.
7. Damage hints display accurate damage values during real battles, matching the existing Practice Ground style.
8. Combat hints display Blocked above the blocking fighter.
9. Combat hints display Deflected above the actual redirected object.
10. Combat hints display Stunned above a fighter who receives a genuine immobilizing effect, such as #0001’s paralysis, but never for regular hitstun.
11. Feedback supports both sides of a battle, including CPU fighters and Watch Mode.
12. Floating messages remain readable, correctly positioned, and free from excessive overlap.
13. All new text works in English and French.
14. Existing combat mechanics, controls, visuals, and settings remain intact.
15. Automated tests pass and the features are verified in a browser.

Final Instructions

Implement this as a cohesive, production-ready interface update.

Do not stop at planning, pseudocode, or placeholder methods. Implement the actual features and integrate them into the existing game.

Read the relevant source before editing. Reuse existing functionality wherever possible, especially Practice Ground’s damage numbers, the combat event system, the projectile deflection records, and Alva’s settings infrastructure.

Avoid unrelated refactors.

After implementation, provide a concise summary of the modified files, the completed features, the tests executed, and any remaining limitations.

The final experience should feel native to Alva: minimal, responsive, readable, and consistent with the game’s existing interface.
